207. What are locators?
Simple meaning

A locator is a mechanism used by Selenium to identify a particular web element on a webpage.

For example, if we want to click a Login button, Selenium first needs to locate that button.

Common example
driver.findElement(By.id("loginBtn")).click();

Here:

findElement() → searches for the element
By.id() → locator strategy
"loginBtn" → locator value
Interview answer

“Locators are used in Selenium to identify and locate web elements on a webpage so that we can perform actions like click, enter text, select, or retrieve values.”

208. What are the types of locators?

Selenium provides 8 traditional locator strategies:

ID
Name
Class Name
Tag Name
Link Text
Partial Link Text
CSS Selector
XPath
Example
By.id("username");

By.name("email");

By.className("login-btn");

By.tagName("input");

By.linkText("Login");

By.partialLinkText("Log");

By.cssSelector("#username");

By.xpath("//input[@id='username']");
Interview answer

“The main Selenium locator strategies are ID, Name, Class Name, Tag Name, Link Text, Partial Link Text, CSS Selector and XPath.”

⭐ Important

In real projects, you will mostly use:

ID → CSS → XPath

depending on the application's HTML.

209. ID vs XPath?
ID	XPath
Usually faster/simple	More powerful
Easy to read	Can be complex
Requires unique ID	Can work without ID
Best when stable	Useful for dynamic elements
By.id()	By.xpath()
Example

HTML:

<input id="username" name="user">

ID:

driver.findElement(By.id("username"));

XPath:

driver.findElement(By.xpath("//input[@id='username']"));
Interview answer

“If a stable and unique ID is available, I prefer ID because it is simple and maintainable. I use XPath when the element doesn't have a reliable ID or when I need relationships, text matching, or dynamic element handling.”

210. CSS vs XPath?
CSS
driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("#username"));
XPath
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//input[@id='username']"));
Major difference

XPath can navigate relationships such as:

parent
child
ancestor
descendant
following
preceding
sibling

CSS cannot provide the same XPath-style DOM traversal.

Interview answer

“CSS selectors are generally concise and easy to maintain, while XPath provides more flexibility, especially for complex DOM relationships, text-based identification and dynamic elements. I use whichever gives me a stable and maintainable locator.”

211. Which locator do you prefer?

Don't say:

“I always use XPath.”

That's not a good senior-level answer.

Better answer

“My preference is to use the most stable and unique locator available. First I look for a reliable ID or application-specific attribute such as data-testid. If those are not available, I use CSS or XPath depending on the DOM structure. For dynamic elements or elements requiring relationships or text matching, XPath is often useful.”

Locator priority

A practical approach:

Stable ID / data-testid
       ↓
CSS
       ↓
XPath
       ↓
Other locators
212. What is Absolute XPath?

Absolute XPath starts from the root of the HTML document.

Example:

/html/body/div/div/form/input

It represents the complete path from the root.

Problem

If one <div> is added to the page:

/html/body/div/div/form/input

may become:

/html/body/div/div/div/form/input

and your locator can break.

Interview answer

“Absolute XPath starts from the root node and specifies the complete path to an element. I generally avoid it because it is tightly coupled to the DOM structure and is fragile when the UI changes.”

213. What is Relative XPath?

Relative XPath starts from anywhere in the DOM using //.

Example:

//input[@id='username']

Instead of:

/html/body/div/form/input
Interview answer

“Relative XPath starts with // and identifies an element based on its attributes, text, or DOM relationships rather than specifying the complete path from the root. It is more maintainable than absolute XPath.”

214. What is Dynamic XPath?

Dynamic XPath is an XPath designed to locate elements whose attributes or other properties may change dynamically.

Suppose:

<input id="user_12345">

Tomorrow:

<input id="user_67890">

The ID changes.

Instead of:

//input[@id='user_12345']

we can use:

//input[contains(@id,'user_')]
Interview answer

“Dynamic XPath is used when element attributes such as ID or class values are generated dynamically. Instead of depending on the complete changing value, I identify the stable portion using functions like contains(), starts-with(), or other stable attributes.”

215. What is contains() in XPath?

contains() checks whether an attribute or text contains a particular value.

Syntax
//tag[contains(@attribute,'value')]
Example

HTML:

<input id="user_12345">

XPath:

//input[contains(@id,'user_')]
Text example
//button[contains(text(),'Login')]
Interview answer

“I use contains() when only a part of an attribute value or text is stable. It is particularly useful for dynamic IDs or partially changing values.”

216. What is starts-with()?

It checks whether an attribute value starts with a particular value.

HTML:

<input id="user_12345">

XPath:

//input[starts-with(@id,'user_')]
Difference

contains():

//input[contains(@id,'user')]

Means:

user can appear anywhere.

starts-with():

//input[starts-with(@id,'user')]

Means:

ID must start with user.

Interview answer

“starts-with() is useful when the beginning portion of a dynamic attribute remains constant.”

217. What is text()?

text() is used to identify elements based on their visible text.

HTML:

<button>Login</button>

XPath:

//button[text()='Login']
Example
driver.findElement(
    By.xpath("//button[text()='Login']")
).click();
Interview answer

“text() is an XPath function used to locate an element based on its text content.”

218. What is normalize-space()?

This is a very useful interview question.

It removes unnecessary leading/trailing spaces and normalizes multiple spaces.

Suppose:

<button>
    Login
</button>

You can use:

//button[normalize-space()='Login']
Another example
//button[normalize-space(text())='Login']
Why useful?

Sometimes UI text contains:

"   Login   "

instead of:

"Login"
Interview answer

“normalize-space() removes leading and trailing whitespace and normalizes consecutive whitespace characters. I use it when the UI contains inconsistent spaces and I need a reliable text-based XPath.”

219. XPath AND

and means both conditions must be true.

HTML:

<input id="username" name="email">

XPath:

//input[@id='username' and @name='email']

Both must match.

Java
driver.findElement(
    By.xpath("//input[@id='username' and @name='email']")
);
Interview answer

“I use and when multiple conditions must be satisfied for the same element.”

220. XPath OR

or means at least one condition must be true.

//input[@id='username' or @name='email']
Difference
[@id='username' and @name='email']

Both required.

[@id='username' or @name='email']

Either can match.

221. What is Parent XPath?

Parent XPath moves from an element to its immediate parent.

Example:

<div>
    <input id="username">
</div>

From input:

//input[@id='username']/parent::div

Or:

//input[@id='username']/..
Interview answer

“The parent axis is used to locate the immediate parent of an element.”

222. What is Child XPath?

Child XPath identifies elements directly under the current element.

Example:

<div>
    <input>
</div>
//div/child::input

Or:

//div/input
Important

/ generally represents a direct child relationship.

223. What is Ancestor?

An ancestor is any parent, grandparent, great-grandparent, etc.

Example:

html
 └── body
      └── div
           └── form
                └── input

For the input:

//input/ancestor::form

Or:

//input/ancestor::div
Interview answer

“The ancestor axis allows us to move upward through the DOM and locate any parent-level element, not just the immediate parent.”

224. What is Descendant?

Descendant means an element located anywhere below the current element.

Example:

//div[@id='login']/descendant::input

This can find inputs inside the div at any nested level.

Difference

Child:

//div/input

Only direct child.

Descendant:

//div/descendant::input

Can be nested deeper.

225. What is Following?

following identifies nodes that appear after the current element anywhere in the document.

Example:

//input[@id='username']/following::button

This can find a button appearing later in the DOM.

Interview answer

“The following axis selects elements that occur after the current node in document order, excluding descendants.”

226. What is Following-Sibling?

This is extremely common in real automation.

Suppose:

<div>
    <label>Username</label>
    <input id="username">
</div>

From label:

//label[text()='Username']/following-sibling::input
Difference

following:

following::

Can search anywhere after the element.

following-sibling:

following-sibling::

Searches elements sharing the same parent.

Interview answer

“following-sibling is used when the required element is a sibling of the current element and appears after it under the same parent.”

227. What is Preceding?

preceding finds elements appearing before the current element in document order.

Example:

//input[@id='username']/preceding::label
228. What is Preceding-Sibling?

Finds a sibling that appears before the current element.

HTML:

<div>
    <label>Username</label>
    <input id="username">
</div>

From input:

//input[@id='username']/preceding-sibling::label
Easy memory trick
following        → anywhere after
following-sibling → same parent + after

preceding        → anywhere before
preceding-sibling → same parent + before
229. XPath with two attributes

Suppose:

<input id="username" name="email" type="text">

You can use:

//input[@id='username' and @name='email']
Another example
//input[@name='email' and @type='text']
Interview answer

“When one attribute is not sufficiently unique, I combine multiple stable attributes using the and operator.”

230. XPath for dynamic ID

Suppose the application generates:

<input id="user_12345">

and later:

<input id="user_67890">

Use:

//input[contains(@id,'user_')]

Or if the beginning is stable:

//input[starts-with(@id,'user_')]
Better real-world approach

Don't automatically use contains().

First check whether there is a stable attribute:

<input
    id="user_12345"
    data-testid="username">

Then:

//*[@data-testid='username']

is better.

⭐ Senior answer

“For dynamic IDs, I first look for a stable application-specific attribute such as data-testid. If that's not available, I use the stable portion of the dynamic ID with contains() or starts-with().”

231. XPath for Gmail/Login link

Suppose the page contains:

<a href="/login">Login</a>
By exact text
//a[text()='Login']
By href
//a[@href='/login']
By partial text
//a[contains(text(),'Login')]
More robust
//a[normalize-space()='Login']
Interview answer

“For a login link, I would first inspect the DOM and prefer a stable unique attribute. If the link has reliable text, I can use //a[normalize-space()='Login']; if the href is stable, I can use the href attribute.”

⭐ Important

Don't blindly give:

//a[text()='Login']

in an interview.

Explain how you choose the locator based on stability and uniqueness.

232. XPath for table row

Suppose:

<table>
  <tr>
    <td>101</td>
    <td>John</td>
    <td>Active</td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>102</td>
    <td>David</td>
    <td>Inactive</td>
  </tr>
</table>

Find the row containing David:

//tr[td[text()='David']]

Or:

//tr[td[normalize-space()='David']]
Selenium
WebElement row = driver.findElement(
    By.xpath("//tr[td[normalize-space()='David']]")
);
⭐ Very important real-world pattern
//tr[td[normalize-space()='David']]

means:

Find the tr where one of its td elements contains David.

233. XPath for specific column

Suppose:

ID	Name	Status
101	John	Active
102	David	Inactive
Find David's Status
//tr[td[normalize-space()='David']]/td[3]

This means:

Find row containing David
        ↓
Go to third td
        ↓
Status
If you want the text
String status = driver.findElement(
    By.xpath("//tr[td[normalize-space()='David']]/td[3]")
).getText();

System.out.println(status);
⭐ Better dynamic table approach

If column position can change, don't hardcode [3].

You can identify the column from the header and then use the appropriate position dynamically.

That's a common 5-year SDET/client-round discussion.

234. What are CSS selectors?

CSS selectors are patterns used to identify HTML elements using CSS-style syntax.

Example:

#username

or:

input[name='email']

Selenium:

driver.findElement(
    By.cssSelector("input[name='email']")
);
Common CSS selectors
#id
.class
tag
tag#id
tag.class
[attribute='value']
235. CSS ID selector

For:

<input id="username">

CSS:

#username

Selenium:

driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("#username"));
Interview answer

“In CSS, # is used to identify an element by its ID.”

236. CSS Class selector

For:

<button class="login-btn">

CSS:

.login-btn

Selenium:

driver.findElement(
    By.cssSelector(".login-btn")
);
Multiple classes
<button class="btn primary login">

You can use:

.btn.primary.login

No spaces.

Important
.btn .primary

means something different — .primary is a descendant.

237. CSS Attribute selector

HTML:

<input type="text" name="username">

CSS:

input[name='username']

Selenium:

driver.findElement(
    By.cssSelector("input[name='username']")
);
Multiple attributes
input[type='text'][name='username']
238. CSS starts-with

CSS uses:

^=
Example
<input id="user_12345">

CSS:

input[id^='user_']

Meaning:

ID starts with user_.

Selenium
driver.findElement(
    By.cssSelector("input[id^='user_']")
);
Remember
^=  → starts with
$=  → ends with
*=  → contains
239. CSS contains

CSS uses:

*=

Example:

<input id="user_12345">
input[id*='user_']

Meaning:

ID contains user_.

CSS ends-with

Also remember:

input[id$='12345']

means:

ID ends with 12345.

