⭐ One question you are VERY likely to get

Interviewer:

“How do you handle a dynamic XPath?”

Strong answer:

“First I inspect the DOM and identify which attributes are stable. I prefer a unique and application-specific attribute such as data-testid if available. If the ID or another attribute is dynamic, I identify the stable portion using XPath functions such as contains() or starts-with(). I also avoid absolute XPath because it is tightly coupled to the DOM structure. My goal is always to create a unique, stable and maintainable locator.”

That answer sounds much stronger than simply saying “I use contains.”

⭐ Another client-round question

Interviewer:

“Your XPath was working yesterday, but today it is failing. What will you do?”

Answer:

“I would first inspect the current DOM and compare it with the previous locator assumptions. I would check whether the element's ID, class, text, hierarchy, iframe, shadow DOM, or page state has changed. I would also check the failure screenshot and logs. If the locator is unstable, I would update it using a more stable attribute or relationship rather than simply adding waits or making the XPath more complex.”

This is the real SDET mindset: don't just make the locator pass; make it stable and maintainable.



